faq:
  - q: "How hard is it to get a job at Google?"
    a: "Google receives millions of applications per year and hires roughly 30,000–40,000 people annually — an acceptance rate well under 1%. The difficulty varies significantly by role: software engineering at L5+ is extremely competitive; program management, finance, and operations roles are more accessible but still rigorous. The interview process is structured, multi-round, and calibrated on a global scale. Preparation matters more than raw talent — candidates who prepare for Google's specific interview format significantly outperform those who rely on general experience."
  - q: "What is Google's interview process?"
    a: "For most roles: (1) Recruiter screen (30 min) — background, role fit, compensation range. (2) Phone or online assessment — coding challenge for engineering roles, role-specific assessment for others. (3) Onsite or virtual interview loop — typically 4–5 rounds of 45–60 minutes each, covering technical skills, behavioral questions using the Googleyness and Leadership framework, and role-specific scenarios. (4) Hiring committee review — all feedback goes to a committee who makes the hire/no-hire decision. (5) Offer and team matching. The full process typically takes 4–8 weeks."
  - q: "What does Google look for in candidates?"
    a: "Four attributes assessed in every interview: (1) General Cognitive Ability — structured problem-solving and learning ability (not just knowledge). (2) Role-Related Knowledge — specific skills for the function. (3) Leadership — how you've driven outcomes through influence, not just authority. (4) Googleyness — a combination of intellectual humility, comfort with ambiguity, collaboration, and alignment with Google's culture. These four attributes are scored independently in every interview round and averaged in the hiring committee review."
  - q: "Do I need a referral to get a job at Google?"
    a: "A referral significantly increases your chances of getting an initial screen — estimates suggest referred candidates are 3–4x more likely to be interviewed than non-referred ones. But a referral does not change the bar; it changes the probability of getting into the process. If you know a Googler, ask them to submit a referral after reviewing your resume. If you don't, a strong application through Google Careers, combined with a direct recruiter message on LinkedIn, can achieve similar results."
  - q: "How should I prepare for Google interviews?"
    a: "For software engineering: master data structures and algorithms (LeetCode, Blind 75, Neetcode), system design at your target level, and behavioral questions using the STAR format anchored to Google's four attributes. For non-engineering roles: master behavioral interviews using STAR examples that demonstrate ownership, analytical thinking, and cross-functional influence. For all roles: research the specific team and product you are interviewing for — interviewers expect genuine interest, not generic enthusiasm."
